#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553830 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ece599e-7211-53e4-b852-c09c4aef59f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-3=\\frac{1}{4}(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553811} {:answer/id #uuid "5072b8c8-db19-54cc-aab6-49d577cbbf16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-3=-\\frac{1}{4}(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553816} {:answer/id #uuid "b5310c74-6e0b-51a9-aff4-4d53c2d25c98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+3=-\\frac{1}{4}(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553832} {:answer/id #uuid "b67dcb0a-7a53-5b01-b1e6-05915ba430aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-3=-4(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553833} {:answer/id #uuid "cb045f3e-c0a3-548a-bc9e-f97458d3c0cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-3=-\\frac{1}{4}(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5072b8c8-db19-54cc-aab6-49d577cbbf16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-3=-\\frac{1}{4}(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553816} :answer-field/id #uuid "584a63db-59f3-5c89-94e7-c2d1bdc0d843" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a69cf94a-f746-5a19-9f67-7603835405a5" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4 t-1,\\qquad y=-2 t^{3}+5 t,\\qquad -\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4 (1)-1=4-1=3 \\\\ y & =-2 (1)^{3}+5 (1)=-2+5=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(3,3).$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=4,\\qquad y'(t)=-6 t^{2}+5.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}=\\frac{-6 t^{2}+5}{4}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{-6 (1)^{2}+5}{4}=\\frac{-1}{4}=-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $(x_1,y_1)=(3,3)$ and the slope $m=-\\frac{1}{4}.$ We get\n\n$$\ny-3=-\\frac{1}{4}(x-3).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553839 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32d5c050-22c0-5c3b-b3e9-4b293311efae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-3=-\\frac{7}{3}(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553812} {:answer/id #uuid "53047d7f-e530-5d0a-aba3-c4a05ecabb3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-3=-\\frac{7}{3}(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553818} {:answer/id #uuid "82675b58-ae0e-59ed-a375-557f1aa20546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-3=-\\frac{3}{7}(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553824} {:answer/id #uuid "9dab7e5a-245a-5e90-968a-9b67cfdbad83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+3=-\\frac{7}{3}(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553828} {:answer/id #uuid "feb93165-8834-5d23-8cb1-cb349bcdd0ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-3=\\frac{7}{3}(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53047d7f-e530-5d0a-aba3-c4a05ecabb3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-3=-\\frac{7}{3}(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553818} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0e7b22c-1e40-526f-a3dd-f397ba590b96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe828f0d-b796-5138-8bc4-7649d4f442dc" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=-3 t+5,\\qquad y=2 t^{3}+t,\\qquad -\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-3 (1)+5=-3+5=2 \\\\ y & =2 (1)^{3}+(1)=2+1=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(2,3).$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=-3,\\qquad y'(t)=6 t^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}=\\frac{6 t^{2}+1}{-3}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{6 (1)^{2}+1}{-3}=\\frac{7}{-3}=-\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $(x_1,y_1)=(2,3)$ and the slope $m=-\\frac{7}{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\ny-3=-\\frac{7}{3}(x-2).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554629 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fa26e4c-314f-5b70-a15a-1830ec32c897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553924} {:answer/id #uuid "61157c7e-b646-58f6-a043-51dc356ac96d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554058} {:answer/id #uuid "8e59702b-e79a-5b04-9309-46a6ac9eac76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554173} {:answer/id #uuid "bcf09cf2-93d1-5582-bc68-59720e36e72f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554277} {:answer/id #uuid "f58d9465-726d-5a95-9c7d-efc84289b65d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcf09cf2-93d1-5582-bc68-59720e36e72f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554277} :answer-field/id #uuid "64fabadb-f80e-5526-b940-02e3d7342ff0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554470}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e6203f86-9cf0-5174-84bf-8575e36ffa78" :question/math-academy-id "q-127310" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨{e}^{2y}+4x,\\,{x}^{7}-{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square $-1\\le x\\le 0,-1\\le y\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/09/09acd3df62b333bdaeb7553599507d4dbad7412ef2770b00e8fc8855b38a774e.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{2y}+4x$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{7}-{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=4,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(4-2y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(4-2y)[x]_{-1}^{0}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(4-2y)\\cdot 1\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(4-2y)\\,dy \\\\  & =[4y-{y}^{2}]_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(4(-1)-(-1)^{2}) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554750 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01e8aae2-003e-59d4-9f51-6280d59e1f6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554751}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01e8aae2-003e-59d4-9f51-6280d59e1f6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554751} :answer-field/id #uuid "b81326d1-592e-5dc9-ace3-bf8cccf3c8ec" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554765} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d8a6047-d4db-5e7d-bc35-ab461c2bccd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d8a6047-d4db-5e7d-bc35-ab461c2bccd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554757} :answer-field/id #uuid "df8790b5-8bb3-538f-a840-1ca0e2d1db38" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "014ba384-9efa-58d4-8e01-62ced760018d" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}-8t,\\,y=-{t}^{3}-6{t}^{2}-9t,\\,-\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Give exact answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if $y'(t)=0$ while $x'(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx'(t)={t}^{2}-2t-8,\\qquad y'(t)=-3{t}^{2}-12t-9.\n$$\n\nSolving $y'(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{t}^{2}-12t-9 & =0 \\\\ -3(t+1)(t+3) & =0 \\\\ t & =-3,-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(-3) & =7\\ne 0, \\\\ x'(-1) & =-5\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=-3$ and $t=-1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554758 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e3aefa0-3c97-5c28-89c2-9c4da8965c43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e3aefa0-3c97-5c28-89c2-9c4da8965c43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554753} :answer-field/id #uuid "3239d979-9412-599c-bf13-cf6040c94d87" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554754} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "101c6d23-9011-58fd-9edb-d075ec501e2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "101c6d23-9011-58fd-9edb-d075ec501e2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554752} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf995db9-cac7-5062-9da3-ca1256276995" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6ec681ae-b1cb-54f1-95d3-8f457841921f" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}-6t,\\,y=-2{t}^{3}+18{t}^{2}-48t,\\,-\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Give exact answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if $y'(t)=0$ while $x'(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=-2{t}^{2}-8t-6,\\qquad y'(t)=-6{t}^{2}+36t-48.\n$$\n\nSolving $y'(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6{t}^{2}+36t-48 & =0 \\\\ -6(t-4)(t-2) & =0 \\\\ t & =2,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(2) & =-30\\ne 0, \\\\ x'(4) & =-70\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=2$ and $t=4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554759 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5898c8d-7eb0-5c79-8e7d-52607c8d64ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5898c8d-7eb0-5c79-8e7d-52607c8d64ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554764} :answer-field/id #uuid "385dd9b4-d451-5634-82e9-e816876b5027" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554755} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ffa7bb79-984f-54ac-93b2-4c15a173aad1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffa7bb79-984f-54ac-93b2-4c15a173aad1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554774} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff41e5b1-a25d-5ea2-b2af-f1f2688e84e9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "752110ff-d39d-57c5-a96b-9123040d4cb8" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-16t,\\,y=2{t}^{3}-6{t}^{2}-18t,\\,-\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Give exact answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if $y'(t)=0$ while $x'(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx'(t)={t}^{2}-16,\\qquad y'(t)=6{t}^{2}-12t-18.\n$$\n\nSolving $y'(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{t}^{2}-12t-18 & =0 \\\\ 6(t-3)(t+1) & =0 \\\\ t & =-1,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(-1) & =-15\\ne 0, \\\\ x'(3) & =-7\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=-1$ and $t=3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554766 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6862b69-924c-5bfb-b51c-6174266c9ded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554770}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6862b69-924c-5bfb-b51c-6174266c9ded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554770} :answer-field/id #uuid "7877e8a5-ece2-5b45-a09e-866686e5f391" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554760} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f496004-c77c-54f4-b7db-0caedac3bca7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f496004-c77c-54f4-b7db-0caedac3bca7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554761} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4e1ea25-fbba-5fba-acdb-34806cf4fd41" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bb8d2f48-ce52-596a-869e-216159bd67da" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3}+32t,\\,y=-{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+9t,\\,-\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Give exact answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if $y'(t)=0$ while $x'(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=-2{t}^{2}+32,\\qquad y'(t)=-3{t}^{2}-6t+9.\n$$\n\nSolving $y'(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{t}^{2}-6t+9 & =0 \\\\ -3(t-1)(t+3) & =0 \\\\ t & =-3,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(-3) & =14\\ne 0, \\\\ x'(1) & =30\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=-3$ and $t=1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554769 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "449eb05f-0eb9-526c-953c-ff1dcf5569bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "449eb05f-0eb9-526c-953c-ff1dcf5569bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554756} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e4d6224-315e-5264-ae6c-6736bd08b681" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554763} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90dab570-d4d5-5d14-95e8-cf430ba68a9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90dab570-d4d5-5d14-95e8-cf430ba68a9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554762} :answer-field/id #uuid "f369480f-edf6-52d7-aeb5-a7f4d6b2804a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554772}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d4f121dc-20d5-5eda-9d00-aee680d617e5" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+12t,\\,y={t}^{3}-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}-6t,\\,-\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Give exact answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if $y'(t)=0$ while $x'(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=-2{t}^{2}+2t+12,\\qquad y'(t)=3{t}^{2}-3t-6.\n$$\n\nSolving $y'(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}-3t-6 & =0 \\\\ 3(t-2)(t+1) & =0 \\\\ t & =-1,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(-1) & =8\\ne 0, \\\\ x'(2) & =8\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=-1$ and $t=2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555770 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3768d3f5-e479-570a-8f87-054f14151075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{25-r}}rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555118} {:answer/id #uuid "3f831b9d-82b6-5349-872b-718dde1c3078" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{16}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{25-{r}^{2}}}z\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555138} {:answer/id #uuid "d1eb0318-10db-5f50-b75e-ea3adc67bc95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{25-{r}^{2}}}z\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555497} {:answer/id #uuid "e0cec740-2b57-59fd-94c7-ffbf1bda6bb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{25-{r}^{2}}}rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555550} {:answer/id #uuid "f4d014df-66b9-5abe-ae05-fa223574e036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{16}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{25-r}}rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555606}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0cec740-2b57-59fd-94c7-ffbf1bda6bb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{25-{r}^{2}}}rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555550} :answer-field/id #uuid "00cb030e-7557-507e-9d73-d9dfccad2a51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "20069222-ef1d-5746-8188-77095ae40eed" :question/math-academy-id "q-161603" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=\\sqrt{25-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}z\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/5d/5d8a94cd0df8095c78aec955c3d0f062e38410326399104644b24e981eb1db9a.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=\\sqrt{25-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}=\\sqrt{25-{r}^{2}}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{25-{r}^{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}zr\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{25-{r}^{2}}}rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555773 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d8fc843-895c-53f1-9b75-77dc01bfca72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4}^{3}rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555026} {:answer/id #uuid "24acc80e-45d9-597e-8b1f-ceca39e70c74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4}^{3}{r}^{2}z\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555073} {:answer/id #uuid "26cae292-83e2-5ef9-a6b6-284121035e4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4}^{3}rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555081} {:answer/id #uuid "8465f297-b1de-54df-9fd9-0bae69bd3f2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4}^{3}rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555305} {:answer/id #uuid "acea55ef-1d37-58bf-91f3-827f6515fd31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4}^{3}z\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555416}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8465f297-b1de-54df-9fd9-0bae69bd3f2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4}^{3}rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555305} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8da3860-d059-5229-bff4-f91bfdfe7bd7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22f18443-8c16-5b1a-8aa4-cdb902d79223" :question/math-academy-id "q-161628" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4,\\,y-4\\le z\\le 3\\},\n$$\n\nthen $$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}z\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/a6/a6b6faca2cd9cb66d463178a24efeb6f47d1dfa0bffe17c657e0d0be2dd11426.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(2,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=y-4=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2)}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}zr\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4}^{3}rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555811 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28bb47d7-b3b3-5032-b1dc-d1d4e63350e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3}^{3}r({r}^{2}+1)\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555089} {:answer/id #uuid "3a455c36-c220-5d88-9049-786a2cd87b97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3}^{3}r({r}^{2}+1)\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555125} {:answer/id #uuid "c09e8eb0-74cf-5f58-b58e-f6c62145cf17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3}^{3}r({r}^{2}+1)\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555462} {:answer/id #uuid "f2223b8c-76e8-51c0-95d5-93e8f8aaa94e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3}^{3}{r}^{2}+1\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555596} {:answer/id #uuid "fc0ff70e-dd05-5b07-bcd7-7e5f6f3c612f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3}^{3}{r}^{2}+1\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a455c36-c220-5d88-9049-786a2cd87b97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3}^{3}r({r}^{2}+1)\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555125} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b11c795-3f01-5752-821c-85dc64d02b46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555682}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5d6469f2-3310-5668-93b9-289e45866c22" :question/math-academy-id "q-161631" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}\\le 4,\\,-y-3\\le z\\le 3\\},\n$$\n\nthen $$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/b1/b1886315cede967bc6fd606cdf7bf9d6ae4f82264ccce22211562aa590a8fe4a.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,-2)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-y-3=-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation ${x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2)}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +4r{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}({(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+1)\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}({r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+1)\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3}^{3}r({r}^{2}+1)\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555819 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25533cdc-be79-58c6-ab12-68206d98ec66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{1}^{{r}^{2}+4}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555075} {:answer/id #uuid "8b5ecda2-04fc-57ef-8508-76f7387c82cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{1}^{{r}^{2}+4}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555326} {:answer/id #uuid "b0761522-850c-5049-b68e-e7c671bc3e19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{1}^{{r}^{2}+4}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555423} {:answer/id #uuid "b86b244b-9164-5eba-9967-5ba9b71c70fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{1}^{r+4}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555439} {:answer/id #uuid "d6399ea9-d979-5a2c-a0ae-56134f1964db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{1}^{r+4}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0761522-850c-5049-b68e-e7c671bc3e19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{1}^{{r}^{2}+4}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555423} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3ce3bff-654b-53ac-b60d-98e14aab9c6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555741}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d03cab7-f35e-5f26-85d3-e871e46cdf45" :question/math-academy-id "q-161609" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2$ for $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ge 0,$ between the surfaces $z=1$ and $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2xy\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/14/140ac7f7d6036657a4ba8a0c6944474c2aa0dfba7f7719228cdffb4d0cf9316f.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-quarter of a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=1,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4={r}^{2}+4.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,0\\le \\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,1\\le z\\le {r}^{2}+4\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}2xy\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}2(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{1}^{{r}^{2}+4}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555841 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b4012ca-c40d-5331-ab80-f5f541c94add" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{2}^{4+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555093} {:answer/id #uuid "3d3cd1d4-431a-5fb2-83e3-87f48ccd6d71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{2}^{4+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555130} {:answer/id #uuid "658b97a0-eabd-507b-8d2d-737539308798" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{2}^{4}r{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555229} {:answer/id #uuid "798db401-1df6-59c8-ae28-251864596785" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{2}^{4+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555282} {:answer/id #uuid "fb84cfdc-3789-5915-a5fd-5ed0d14ca0e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{r}^{2}{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "798db401-1df6-59c8-ae28-251864596785" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{2}^{4+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555282} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad4e7b48-b967-5cd8-8d67-9aafb4784107" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9ff4f4d5-934d-5904-8c7d-edf6b1d6934c" :question/math-academy-id "q-161602" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ between the surfaces $z=2$ and $z=4+y.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/28/286910d89e35603dc6558b80b11d06736d213fbad8c9c91825f42ff87b26e318.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{5}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=2,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=4+y=4+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{5},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\,2\\le z\\le 4+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{z}^{2}r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{2}^{4+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555859 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "347c2f0c-5a52-5eed-a5f6-845a1b198245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555111} {:answer/id #uuid "48db9c66-7f10-5f23-8a17-8771c3a07517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555157} {:answer/id #uuid "95cbbbb8-182c-54d0-b493-2f62f281a0dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555357} {:answer/id #uuid "db217f00-30c9-50d9-a7c1-29d4b1d834be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555531} {:answer/id #uuid "dbc989c2-ca66-5d8e-9dc2-695894977ce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555533}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48db9c66-7f10-5f23-8a17-8771c3a07517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555157} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cfeab61-6e76-5a26-9da7-d3d287d0e865" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d96a4476-f395-552e-94ef-9cd8de4e9f95" :question/math-academy-id "q-54292" :question/problem "![](images/ac/ac28d44b12c5804a36cc9ae7a3655237449bfe5f1933df81d70ece5ee18ed981.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nI. $y=-4x+2$\n\nII. $y=x+4$\n\nIII. $y=-2x+4$" :question/worked-solution "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,4)$ and $(2,0)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/47/4719dc10bbf4078e6b266a36b50a82e117b3e775395a8a3e44c0dff4ae56f15b.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y=-4x+2.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) & =-4(0)+2 \\\\ 4 & =2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-4x+2$ is *not* equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation II: $y=x+4.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$ and $B(2,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) & =(0)+4 \\\\ 4 & =4\\,✓ \\\\ (0) & =(2)+4 \\\\ 0 & =6\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=x+4$ is *not* equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $y=-2x+4.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$ and $B(2,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) & =-2(0)+4 \\\\ 4 & =4\\,✓ \\\\ (0) & =-2(2)+4 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-2x+4$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equation that represented the line in the graph was $y=-2x+4,$ corresponding to answer choice III. Therefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556215 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ae87d4f-d71a-56f5-a511-d52b7388140a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta + 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556239} {:answer/id #uuid "73ec0a2c-d4ea-506d-aea6-38ea96ab4ad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta - 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556247} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0afd26-2b25-5b36-b0d7-b3432052facf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta - 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556273} {:answer/id #uuid "d39f59a4-e243-52e6-b07d-0ee6530350e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556278} {:answer/id #uuid "e3714180-108c-5f1c-9483-03926dfa877b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 6 \\sin^{2} \\theta + 7 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73ec0a2c-d4ea-506d-aea6-38ea96ab4ad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta - 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556247} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f003a65-bfb4-524e-a657-98d8efa2df73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "17340d7a-f7d9-5af9-9d56-d06b4143d831" :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\sin \\theta(4\\cos \\theta-6\\sin \\theta)+\\cos \\theta(3\\sin \\theta-6\\cos \\theta).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\sin \\theta(4\\cos \\theta-6\\sin \\theta)+\\cos \\theta(3\\sin \\theta-6\\cos \\theta) = 4\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-6\\sin^{2}\\theta+3\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-6\\cos^{2}\\theta\n$$\n\nNext, we group the $\\sin^2 \\theta$ and $\\cos^2 \\theta$ terms.\n\n$$\n4\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-6\\sin^{2}\\theta+3\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-6\\cos^{2}\\theta = 7\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-6(\\sin^{2}\\theta+\\cos^{2}\\theta)\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^2{\\theta}+\\cos^2{\\theta}=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n7\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-6(\\sin^{2}\\theta+\\cos^{2}\\theta) = 7 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta - 6\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sin \\theta(4\\cos \\theta-6\\sin \\theta)+\\cos \\theta(3\\sin \\theta-6\\cos \\theta) = 7 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta - 6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556225 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18ed5be4-3d45-545a-9d52-a05004e5beac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta + 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556216} {:answer/id #uuid "3231ddc1-35cf-5f76-9671-f3021df53283" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556227} {:answer/id #uuid "496ef258-197f-5b8c-a044-63e42b9882ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 4 \\sin^{2} \\theta + 5 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556235} {:answer/id #uuid "6817befa-fcdd-52fb-bbe7-b514774c3c77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta - 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556241} {:answer/id #uuid "c0b41444-013f-5627-a027-c7f1a88a1b82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 5 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta - 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6817befa-fcdd-52fb-bbe7-b514774c3c77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta - 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556241} :answer-field/id #uuid "44441b23-220b-5075-948e-5c96be268dca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2f8a92d4-c324-5170-939e-341e5ba8eec8" :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\sin \\theta(5\\cos \\theta-4\\sin \\theta)-4\\cos^{2}\\theta.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\sin \\theta(5\\cos \\theta-4\\sin \\theta)-4\\cos^{2}\\theta = 5\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-4\\sin^{2}\\theta-4\\cos^{2}\\theta\n$$\n\nNext, we group the $\\sin^2 \\theta$ and $\\cos^2 \\theta$ terms.\n\n$$\n5\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-4\\sin^{2}\\theta-4\\cos^{2}\\theta = 5\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-4(\\sin^{2}\\theta+\\cos^{2}\\theta)\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^2{\\theta}+\\cos^2{\\theta}=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n5\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-4(\\sin^{2}\\theta+\\cos^{2}\\theta) = 5 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta - 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sin \\theta(5\\cos \\theta-4\\sin \\theta)-4\\cos^{2}\\theta = 5 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta - 4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556226 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fc45ad5-b723-5e96-9b91-e584de3e4945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 4 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta - 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556242} {:answer/id #uuid "83c951fc-884f-5356-a1b7-8564f381faf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 6 \\sin^{2} \\theta + 4 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556249} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f66597-1fbe-5643-ab86-fd4b5ee5de45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta - 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556261} {:answer/id #uuid "a62fabe4-e16f-5389-88ab-1087aa9569ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556262} {:answer/id #uuid "abedba6e-3fc5-5677-95b8-b73747f411cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta + 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0f66597-1fbe-5643-ab86-fd4b5ee5de45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta - 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556261} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c71da57-5c7e-5a0a-a1ec-03aeaf91af42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556213}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2f9675c1-b265-56d1-a5d2-0ce163737ab3" :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\sin \\theta(4\\cos \\theta-6\\sin \\theta)-6\\cos^{2}\\theta.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\sin \\theta(4\\cos \\theta-6\\sin \\theta)-6\\cos^{2}\\theta = 4\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-6\\sin^{2}\\theta-6\\cos^{2}\\theta\n$$\n\nNext, we group the $\\sin^2 \\theta$ and $\\cos^2 \\theta$ terms.\n\n$$\n4\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-6\\sin^{2}\\theta-6\\cos^{2}\\theta = 4\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-6(\\sin^{2}\\theta+\\cos^{2}\\theta)\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^2{\\theta}+\\cos^2{\\theta}=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n4\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-6(\\sin^{2}\\theta+\\cos^{2}\\theta) = 4 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta - 6\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sin \\theta(4\\cos \\theta-6\\sin \\theta)-6\\cos^{2}\\theta = 4 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta - 6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556236 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26409c77-3313-5dc4-80b5-91d079c09750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556219} {:answer/id #uuid "272c357d-71a8-569a-8654-f2a5f4137991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556220} {:answer/id #uuid "374beb1e-85b2-58a4-802b-e7a101b1677e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556229} {:answer/id #uuid "51b83310-87ae-5ef1-a647-3d6de8f81daa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556237} {:answer/id #uuid "7945ec28-8bb9-584c-bfc0-5a740ea9e397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556248}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51b83310-87ae-5ef1-a647-3d6de8f81daa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556237} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab5cbfbf-428d-5d59-8155-34ff2d2225cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "517a8107-290f-5a84-8f6a-2ed0ba79a829" :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $(4\\sin \\theta-4\\cos \\theta)^{2}+(4\\sin \\theta+4\\cos \\theta)^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n(4\\sin \\theta-4\\cos \\theta)^{2}+(4\\sin \\theta+4\\cos \\theta)^{2} = 16\\sin^{2}\\theta-32\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta+16\\cos^{2}\\theta+16\\sin^{2}\\theta+32\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta+16\\cos^{2}\\theta\n$$\n\nNext, we group the $\\sin^2 \\theta$ and $\\cos^2 \\theta$ terms.\n\n$$\n16\\sin^{2}\\theta-32\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta+16\\cos^{2}\\theta+16\\sin^{2}\\theta+32\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta+16\\cos^{2}\\theta = 32(\\sin^{2}\\theta+\\cos^{2}\\theta)\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^2{\\theta}+\\cos^2{\\theta}=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n32(\\sin^{2}\\theta+\\cos^{2}\\theta) = 32\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(4\\sin \\theta-4\\cos \\theta)^{2}+(4\\sin \\theta+4\\cos \\theta)^{2} = 32.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556250 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d0ee518-2b8e-5cd4-a0c6-501e8b4c4c30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 3 \\sin^{2} \\theta + 9 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556223} {:answer/id #uuid "9af850a6-e9d5-53bf-af75-fa82fd4cac16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta - 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556258} {:answer/id #uuid "c1e90331-97df-5557-b6f0-e2b8d7dbec6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556271} {:answer/id #uuid "ecb4649a-6430-5f6e-800f-8d80dee0a49c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9 